585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The primary drawback in the proposed Bill aimed at replacing the IPC is the)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
586 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (complete removal of Section 377 of the IPC, without establishing alternative mechanisms for addressing sexual offences involving both humans and animals. Undoubtedly, Section 377 has its origins in colonial-era moral values that deemed same-sex relationships as criminal. However, the absence of any legislation to address sexual crimes against animals creates a vacuum, potentially leading to an increase in such offences. Eliminating the colonial-era statute could have had a significant impact if the )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
587 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 338 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
588 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (proposed Bill had introduced a gender-neutral rape law and included a separate provision for addressing sexual offenses against animals, recognizing them as victims of such crimes.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
589 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (MARITAL RAPE IS NOT EXPLICITLY ADDRESSED IN THE BNS)
/E (MARITAL RAPE IS NOT EXPLICITLY ADDRESSED IN THE BNS)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
590 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (There is no provision for marital rape in the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita of 2023. Both Exception 2 in Clause 63 \(rape\) of the Bill and Exception 2 in Section 375 of the IPC essentially maintain the same principle sexual intercourse by a man with his own wife is not considered rape, with only a difference in the specified age limit.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
591 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
