601 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita \(BNS\) brings in stricter and more extensive provisions for addressing offences against women and children, dedicating a whole chapter to these matters. In contrast, the Indian Penal Code \(IPC\) lumps such offences under a chapter that deals with crimes "affecting the human body." Nevertheless, the language and tone of potential revisions within the BNS appear to adhere to a traditional gender binary perspective, prioritising the preservation of women's honour rather than advancing gender equality.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
602 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ("We have to take a close look at how we actually apply the new law and make sure it's fair. We need to make sure it doesn't support harmful ideas about how different genders should behave or unfair judgments, just like important court rulings in the past have demonstrated." How we make and understand this law is really important for its success."The provisions encompassed under BNS are commendable, nonetheless, existing loopholes aren't sufficiently addressed and necessitate further exploration.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
