/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The implications of poor drafting are magnified by the new confessions provision in Clause 23 of the Bill. Previously, the Evidence Act's Section 25 prohibited the use of confessions given to the police, whether during custody or not, in court proceedings. This exclusion had a well-recognized policy behind it, articulated by Sir James Stephen, the Act's author. The rationale was to deter police from resorting to coercion to extract confessions. )
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
786 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (23)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
787 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (HAVE ALL THE POTENTIAL VULNERABILITIES AND GAPS BEEN THOROUGHLY MITIGATED?)
/E (HAVE ALL THE POTENTIAL VULNERABILITIES AND GAPS BEEN THOROUGHLY MITIGATED?)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
788 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Consistent with this logic, Section 26 of the Evidence Act established that confessions made while under arrest to individuals other than police officers would be admissible only if they were made in the presence of a magistrate.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
789 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The idea was that if evidence obtained by the police couldn't be used in court, there would be no incentive for them to employ coercive tactics to obtain confessions.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
790 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Although the previously mentioned restrictions were in place, Section 27 of the Act, following these clauses, permitted the admission of specific portions of confessions called "discovery statements" in court if relevant items \(such as the offender's weapon\) were subsequently found. This provision has faced considerable criticism for potentially reducing the initial deterrent effect against the use of coercion to obtain confessions.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
